    "final_text": "All the problems in INC-5521 are fixed in `src/inventory.js`. The full suite passes (17 tests). I did not break the code on purpose to confirm each new test fails against the old version.\n\n**Causes:**\n- **Read-modify-write races.** Every method read a counter, yielded to the store, then wrote it back, so concurrent workers overwrote each other's updates. That explains the oversell (263 orders on 250 units), `sold` counts that were too high, and `reserved` going negative. `addStock` had the same problem.\n- **Retry held stock twice.** `reserve` checked for an existing order and then wrote it much later. Two overlapping retries with the same `orderId` both got through and each held the stock.\n- **Bundle left half-held.** `reserve` incremented and saved each SKU as it went, so an out-of-stock second item threw after the first was already held.\n- **`release` and `expire` mishandled committed orders.** `release` accepted committed orders and decremented `reserved` again. `expire()` released every old order, including committed ones. That is the likely source of the negative `reserved` and the extra `sold` after the nightly job.\n\n**Fix:**\n- **Locking.** I added a small per-key async lock in the same file. `reserve`, `commit` and `release` each lock the order and its SKUs, and `addStock` locks its SKU. Locks are always taken in the same order (order first, then SKUs sorted), so opposite-order bundles can't deadlock.\n- **`reserve`.** The idempotency check and the stock check run under the lock. Every SKU is checked before any is written, so a failed bundle holds nothing.\n- **`release` and `expire`.** `release` now throws `OrderStateError` on a committed order, as its doc comment says. `expire()` skips orders that aren't `reserved` and re-checks status under the lock, so a commit that lands mid-scan wins.\n- **Shared helper.** `commit` and `release` now go through one internal `settle` helper. The public methods, return values and error types are unchanged, and `src/store.js` is untouched.\n\n**Tests:** `tests/concurrency.test.js` has 9 tests covering each report item: oversell, lost `addStock` updates, double-hold on retry, the partial bundle, double commit and double release, release of a committed order, `expire()` against committed and released orders and against a concurrent commit, and deadlock-free opposite-order bundles.\n\n**Assumption:** the lock only covers one `InventoryService` instance. If you run several service processes against one production store, you would need a store-level mechanism such as a Redis script or compare-and-set. I did not build that.",
